WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:02.936
So nine years ago I quit my job as a

00:00:02.936 --> 00:00:04.738
cryonics planner, you know for those people

00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:05.939
who want to freeze themselves for a

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:07.240
thousand years, hoping to wake up in a

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.341
better world?

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.943
My job was to make sure their frozen

00:00:09.943 --> 00:00:11.678
bodies would stay safe and sound for all

00:00:11.678 --> 00:00:12.645
that long...

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.748
No pressure right? I mean how do you even

00:00:14.748 --> 00:00:16.316
design a building to last for a

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:18.585
millennium? What if there's a zombie

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:20.620
apocalypse or a giant meteor? Or another

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.089
Global pandemic? How do you guarantee

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:24.657
your clients won't end up as popsicles

00:00:24.657 --> 00:00:27.660
for some alien invaders?! Or in a zoo?!

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.696
I mean, given that nothing lasts forever,

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:32.699
what is a literally sustainable design?

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:35.101
Well good luck finding any answers on

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:35.869
the Internet.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:37.370
Most of what you'll find a bunch of

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.239
greenwashing products or programs that

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:40.907
claim to be eco-friendly but they're

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.342
actually just as bad if not worse than

00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:44.310
status quo. I mean they promise to have a

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.012
net zero impact but they don't tell you

00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:47.680
how they measure that, or what they do

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.416
with the waste, or how they exploit the

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:51.117
workers, or how they brought the

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.153
politicians. I mean they don't care about

00:00:53.153 --> 00:00:54.521
your future--they primarily care about

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:56.156
their profit.

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:58.124
What just about made me give up though was

00:00:58.124 --> 00:00:59.692
when I was working on a huge map for San

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:01.594
Antonio. A map that showed all the

00:01:01.594 --> 00:01:04.497
infrastructure--planned and the existing.

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.332
A map that revealed a shocking truth:

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:08.568
25% of the pipes underground were

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.904
old rotten. They're leaking, cracking

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.772
bursting, and contaminating the water supply.

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:14.808
They were violating the Clean Water Act

00:01:14.808 --> 00:01:16.743
and the city was facing like a billion

00:01:16.743 --> 00:01:19.679
dollar fine. How do you explain that to

00:01:19.679 --> 00:01:21.648
the voters? How do you tell them they

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.316
have to fork-over over billions of dollars

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.485
for something they never asked for, never see

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:27.220
never care about? How do you tell them

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.856
they have to pay for flushing toilets?

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:31.291
You know, so that's the burning question:

00:01:31.291 --> 00:01:33.359
how do we create a truly sustainable

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.395
human world? You know, world where we don't ruin

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:36.763
everything for ourselves and our kids?

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.698
A world where we don't have to worry about

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:42.702
climate change, pollution, resource depletion, or extinction?

00:01:42.702 --> 00:01:44.270
A world where we can have a harmonious

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.473
story between us of nature?

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:48.641
After a year I was so stressed out by

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.210
this question and I decided to seek some

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:52.378
spiritual guidance. I ended up cooking

00:01:52.378 --> 00:01:54.414
breakfast for this Tibetan monk. He was

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:56.616
very nice, very wise, very... bald.

00:01:56.616 --> 00:01:58.351
But over some scrambled eggs and coffee,

00:01:58.351 --> 00:02:00.286
he asked me what was bothering, me what

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.255
did I want to learn? I babbled on about

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:04.057
my interest in impermanence, fractal

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:06.993
geometry, psychology, storytelling... And he

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:09.362
stopped, and he said "Storytelling...

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:12.832
that's it! that research needs to be done!"

00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:15.768
So I did. I quit my job, packed my

00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:18.338
cats and I moved to Cyprus. Here I read a

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:19.606
lot of books and articles--and I came

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:21.341
across this amazing story about two

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:22.609
Australian researchers who were

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:25.245
following a "songline". Now, a "songline"

00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:27.413
it turns out it's like an epic GPS the

00:02:27.413 --> 00:02:29.315
aboriginal people used to navigate the

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:31.718
land. They sing the stories, and if

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:33.520
they're going in the right direction, the

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.822
landforms along the way correspond to

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:37.557
the characters in the story.

00:02:37.557 --> 00:02:39.659
Okay so the year is 2014, and these

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:41.027
researchers are walking along, singing

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:42.162
their little hearts out when suddenly

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:44.264
they're standing in the sea. They thought

00:02:44.264 --> 00:02:46.733
they messed up somehow. Maybe they took a

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.535
wrong turn at the burning bush?

00:02:48.535 --> 00:02:50.937
But then they realized: maybe this song

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:53.740
continues under the water?

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.775
And sure enough they found the rest of

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.510
the landmarks submerged in the sea on

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:00.346
oceanic charts! This song line was so old

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.615
it dated back to the last-ice age, when

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:04.484
the sea level was that much lower.

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:06.819
This song line had survived for like 10 thousand

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:10.423
500 years--passed down by word of mouth for

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.825
at least 500 Generations!

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.061
Now, that's longer than any cyronics

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:17.330
project ever dreamed of! And probably more

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:18.765
fun...

00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:21.034
Well, this blew my mind: how can we use

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:22.902
this ancient technology to shape our

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:24.704
future? Maybe we could leverage

00:03:24.704 --> 00:03:26.940
continuity going backwards...to go

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.742
forwards. But how can we tell our stories

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:31.110
to the land like that and make them

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.312
stick?

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:34.113
Well, it turns out it's just that simple:

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:36.482
you need to map your stories. You know

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.784
the stories you tell yourself, or your

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:39.652
friends, or your therapists: meaningful

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.454
stories. The stories that make up who you

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.356
are and where you come from. All those

00:03:43.356 --> 00:03:44.857
stories have a setting--and a setting

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:46.292
can be mapped!

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.595
Imagine our stories like a tapestry and

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:50.029
when we lay them out on the land we

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:51.464
could see how the threads of each other's

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:53.199
stories intertwine and how they tug at

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:55.735
each other, how they harmonize.  Now with

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:57.337
this knowledge, we can spin new tales

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:58.771
that build upon the old--you know like

00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:00.406
weaving new threads to fill in the worn

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.208
holes of that tapestry.

00:04:02.208 --> 00:04:04.077
And mapping our stories isn't just for

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:05.511
personal insight. We could use them to

00:04:05.511 --> 00:04:07.080
guide decisions about our neighborhoods:

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.716
climate, immigration, business, development,

00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:11.918
isolation. We can make sure that

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:13.419
everything we do is in harmony with our

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.121
stories--or at least not

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:16.356
contradicting them.

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:18.224
Just to live together we need to

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:20.193
know where each other are coming from!

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:21.761
The aboriginal people of Australia

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.396
showed me that the stories, mixed with

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:25.131
the land, can last for thousands of years.

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:26.933
So using stories of the people here

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.802
in Famagusta, I made a prototype of a map

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:31.738
of stories we and

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:34.674
our descendants could adapt and evolve.

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:36.342
Stories that could last for ten thousand

00:04:36.342 --> 00:04:37.377
years or more.

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:40.013
Now, we all know there are gaps in the

00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:42.348
structures that support our society. In

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:44.083
order to enable communities to plug

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:46.052
those gaps, we need to build a narrative infrastructure now.

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:48.421
One that maps stories

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:50.623
and communities across the world. 

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.326
With narrative tools and GIS, we can create a

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:54.827
"narrative infrastructure" for the future.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:57.697
A future that's not on ice, but alive

00:04:57.697 --> 00:04:59.499
with stories.

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:01.000
Thank you very much.

